# lscale

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

#' Compute L-Scale Estimator (Maronna's Method)
#'
#' @description
#' Computes the L-scale estimator as defined by Maronna (2005) for robust
#' multivariate analysis. Used in robust principal component analysis to assess
#' the scale of squared, centered observations.
#'
#' @param x Numeric vector of squared, centered values (must be non-negative).
#' @param m Integer. Dimensionality parameter (typically total number of variables).
#' @param q Integer. Reduction parameter. For PCA reducing to p dimensions,
#'   \code{q = ncol(x) - p}.
#'
#' @return Numeric. The L-scale value (sum of smallest \code{hval} values).
#'
#' @details
#' The L-scale is computed by:
#' \enumerate{
#'   \item Computing \code{hval = floor((length(x) + m - q + 2)/2)}
#'   \item Sorting \code{x} in ascending order
#'   \item Summing the first \code{hval} values
#' }
#'
#' This assumes values in \code{x} have already been:
#' \itemize{
#'   \item Centered (location measure subtracted)
#'   \item Squared
#' }
#'
#' All values must be non-negative; the function stops with an error otherwise.
#'
#' @references
#' Maronna, R.A. (2005). Principal components and orthogonal regression based on
#' robust scales. Technometrics, 47, 264-273.
#'
#' @seealso \code{\link{SPCA}} for robust principal components analysis
#'
#' @keywords multivariate robust
#' @export
lscale<-function(x,m,q)
{
#
# Compute the L-scale as used by Marrona
# Technometrics, 2005, 47, 264-273
#
# so it is assumed that values in x have been centered
# (a measure of location has been subtracted from each value)
# and the results squared.
#
#  q is defined in Marrona. For principal components, want to reduce
#  to p dimensional data, q=ncol(x)-p
#
hval<-floor((length(x)+m-q+2)/2)
flag<-(x<0)
if(sum(flag)>0)stop("For lscale, all values must be nonnegative")
x<-sort(x)
val<-sum(x[1:hval])
val
}




