# quant

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

#' Compute Sample Quantiles (Wrapper for quantile)
#'
#' @description
#' Convenience wrapper for R's \code{\link[stats]{quantile}} function that uses
#' the quantile estimator recommended by Hyndman and Fan (1996) by default.
#'
#' @param x Numeric vector.
#' @param q Numeric vector of probabilities with values in [0,1] (default: 0.5 for median).
#' @param names Logical. If \code{TRUE}, include names in result (default: \code{TRUE}).
#' @param na.rm Logical. If \code{TRUE}, remove NA values (default: \code{TRUE}).
#' @param type Integer 1-9 selecting quantile algorithm (default: 8).
#'   Type 8 is recommended by Hyndman and Fan (1996).
#'
#' @details
#' This function provides a consistent interface matching other WRS package functions
#' while using R's \code{quantile()} function. The default \code{type=8} uses:
#' \deqn{Q(p) = (1-\gamma) x_{(j)} + \gamma x_{(j+1)}}
#' where \eqn{\gamma = (j + 1/3)/(n + 1/3)} and j is the integer part of np + 1/3.
#'
#' This estimator is approximately median-unbiased and performs well for
#' continuous distributions.
#'
#' @return
#' Numeric vector of quantile values. Length equals \code{length(q)}.
#'
#' @references
#' Hyndman, R. J. and Fan, Y. (1996). Sample quantiles in statistical packages.
#' \emph{American Statistician}, \bold{50}, 361-365.
#'
#' @seealso \code{\link[stats]{quantile}}, \code{\link{Quart}}, \code{\link{hd}},
#'   \code{\link{idealf}}
#'
#' @keywords univar
#' @export
quant<-function(x,q=.5,names=TRUE,na.rm=TRUE,type=8){
#
# For convenience, follow style of other functions in Rallfun
# when using the R function quanitle. Also, use by default the estimator
# recommended by Hyndman and  Fan (1996).
#
a=quantile(x,probs=q,names=names,type=type,na.rm=na.rm)
a
}




